  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is happening even before the new laws take \neffect. The first is interest rates. Mortgage  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rates have effectively tripled from where they \nwere when housing peaked. Meaning that by simple  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arithmetic, people just cannot borrow as much as \nthey could a few years ago with the same income.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564400,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second is that internal migration patterns \nhave shifted. People are still moving out of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the pandemic boom cities back towards the few \ncenters with job opportunities now. Again, I have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been yapping about this for three years now, so \nit's probably not exactly shocking to any of you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "watching anymore. But the third and probably the \nmost underappreciated variable is insurance costs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which have become a major deal killer, especially \nin certain regions of the Sunb Belt. To buy a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589440,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "house with a mortgage, you need to hold home \ninsurance. And there are now a lot of parts of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "country that have become borderline uninsurable. \nIn Florida, the average annual home insurance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598160,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "premium hit $8,292 last year, up 18% in a single \nyear. In Houston, homeowners are paying almost  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$8,000 a year. These get counted against people's \nability to repay their mortgage and applications.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because $8,000 going to an insurance company \nis $8,000 that a household won't have to make  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mortgage repayments. A growing chunk of homeowners \nwho can't get coverage through traditional  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "insurance are also being pushed into the surplus \nlines market where premiums are even higher and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consumer protections are weaker. That market went \nfrom handling about 2% of home policies to roughly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "16% in states like Florida and Texas in just 3 \nyears. At those prices, even if someone can afford  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mortgage, they might not be able to afford to \ninsure the house, making it effectively unsellable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to everybody outside of cash buyers who are \nwilling to live in an uninsurable home. On top of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645680,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of this, those very same investment firms that \nhave bought up all of those single family homes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over the last decade were kind of heading for \nthe exit even before this law was passed, trying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get out ahead of what they clearly see as an \nincoming market correction. Large institutional  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "investors are now net sellers of single \nfamily rental homes. In Dallas, for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "investors own about 9% of the housing stock, but \nare responsible for almost 23% of all new for sale  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "listings. So yeah, even before this law goes into \neffect, the housing market has got rates keeping  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buyers sidelined, insurance making entire regions \nunaffordable, and the biggest institutional buyers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dumping inventory. The drops aren't catastrophic \nyet, and almost everywhere is still up compared  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to before co. But if these new laws really do \ndeliver the supply they are promising on top  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of everything that is already pushing prices \ndown, it's worth at least asking what happens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we push things too far in the other direction. \nPotentially, this could kind of be like when your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "insufferable co-workers insist on turning the \noffice into a sauna during the summer and then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "finally lamenting and turning on the AC right \nas we were already heading into winter. I mean,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708560,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's great that they came around, but this would \nhave probably been a lot better to do a few months  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ago. So, it's time to learn how money works to \nfind out if this is the right policy just at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717440,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wrong time and if falling house prices really \nare an economic crisis. Most people have no idea  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how much of their personal information is already \nout there. Hundreds of data brokers are collecting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and selling your data right now, and they don't \nneed your permission to do it. That's why this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "video is sponsored by Incogn. Your email address, \nphone number, home address, and more are probably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sitting on data broker websites right now, ready \nfor scammers and identity thieves to buy. And it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not your fault. It's mostly because the companies \nyou trusted with your data aren't careful enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with it. Incogn automatically contacts these data \nbrokers on your behalf and demands they remove  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753200,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your information. They handle the back and forth, \nthe follow-ups, and the legal requests so you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don't have to. And with their new custom removals \nfeature, they can even go after specific instances  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762880,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of your info being leaked online. Just Google \nyourself, and you might be surprised to find  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that news sites, fitness apps, or random websites \nyou've never heard of are holding and sharing your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "personal details. With custom removals, you just \nsubmit the link and Incogn privacy experts will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "handle the rest. The way I see it, they can't \nsteal your identity if they have no idea who  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781520,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are. Go to incogn.com/howoneyworks or use \ncode howy works at the link below to get 60%  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off an annual plan and take your personal data \nback. Okay, so again, there's a big difference  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between a crisis causing falling prices and \nfalling prices causing a crisis. It's also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just possible for house prices to fall. If \na regular good or service becomes cheaper,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is usually an optimal economic outcome. So, \nwell, what kind of situation are we really looking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at? NPR's Planet Money published a really good \nbreakdown of this exact question last week, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813600,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we will leave a link to it in the description. But \nto steal their homework a bit, the key distinction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "comes down to why prices are falling. If prices \ndrop because more housing is being built and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822960,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supply is catching up to demand, that is a healthy \nmarket doing exactly what markets are supposed to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do. Denver right now is a decent example of this. \nHome prices are down over 2% year-over-year.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rents have dropped even further, and renters \nin the city are understandably celebrating. One  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "29-year-old medical resident who NPR interviewed \njust moved into a bigger apartment with bigger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843120,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amenities that he previously couldn't afford, but \ncan now because rents have come down. His friend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "renegotiated her lease for $500 less a month just \nby showing her landlord what comparable units were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "listing for. The underlying economy in Denver is \nstill growing. People still want to live there.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Land values are stable. The city just actually \nbuilt enough housing to give buyers and renters  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some options and in doing so also gave employment \nopportunity to trade people who were doing the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868080,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "building. That is in theory the good kind of price \ndecline. It sounds great, but then of course there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will be people pointing to counter examples like \nDetroit. After decades of de-industrialization,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the city lost nearly a third of its population \nbetween 1990 and 2010. Home prices fell by over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "80% and at one point in 2007, houses were \nliterally cheaper than cars. It was a pretty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grim situation that was made even worse by the \nglobal financial crisis, which again was a crisis  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causing a decline in prices. Even still, nobody \nwas celebrating affordable housing in Detroit.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was an economy collapsing and dragging real \nestate down with it. And to be clear, throughout  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904400,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modern history, there have been dozens of regional \nhousing corrections that went basically unnoticed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the broader economy. 2008 was the exception, \nnot the rule. So, if we were just building more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "houses and making shelter more affordable, \nthat should be a good thing. Even for people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who already own their own homes outright, broadly \nhigher house prices on a day-to-day basis really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mean higher property taxes and higher insurance \npremiums anyway. If they sell, they still need to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buy somewhere else to live. So lower prices across \nthe board actually reduces their ongoing expenses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without drastically impacting their realizable \nwealth. The fear-mongering around 2008 should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be understood in its proper context here. That \ncrisis was caused by severe structural problems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the financial industry that just happened to be \nusing housing as a convenient conduit for reckless  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speculation. The housing market was fragile, \nyes, but it was collateral damage to the real  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underlying problem, which was that Wall Street had \nbeen bundling garbage mortgages into securities,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stamping them AAA, and spreading those losses \nacross the entire global financial system. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963280,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the widespread FUD from groups who simply don't \nwant the value of their assets to be undermined  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is, to put it generously, a bit disingenuous. \nOne additional factor, for what it's worth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that even for people who do view housing purely \nas an investment, they have to recognize that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their investment returns have largely been propped \nup by very accommodating government policies that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protected the housing market over the last two \ndecades. So, it should stand to reason that a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "private investment that benefited from government \nsupport would also be exposed to the risk of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "government intervention, too. However, there are \nstill genuine risks here. Falling house prices  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "become an actual economic problem when people \nare overleveraged in housing, when the financial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003520,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system is using it as collateral, and when other \nsectors of the economy are dependent on housing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "values continuing to go up. And well, we kind of \ntick all three of those boxes at the moment. Which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brings us to the much less comfortable question of \nwho actually ends up paying for this. If the music  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really does slow down, someone is going to end up \nin a bad position. And overwhelmingly right now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is people who have only just purchased their \nhomes with minimal equity. If you have bought a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "house in the last two or three years, especially \nafter 2002 when rates were already elevated,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you might be sitting on a mortgage at 6 plus% \nwith very little buffer between what you owe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and what the house is currently worth. A 10 or 15% \ndrop from here and you're underwater, owing more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the house could sell for. Now, with a bit of \ncritical thinking, we can probably see that this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048320,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might have been a lot less painful if we hadn't \nspent the last decade trying to get as many people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a home as possible with 1% deposits and \neffectively zero safety buffer. But, say, what's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058800,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more is that even if we did end up seeing price \ndeclines similar to somewhere like New Zealand's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063200,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "major cities, which we probably won't for reasons \nwe will get into soon, that is still only going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to put us back to where we were in 2021. So, \nwe are kind of stuck between a rock and a hard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "place where prices are allowed to grow so much \nthat anything short of a catastrophic drop still  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "won't actually make homes affordable. Now, there \nis some tragic irony in all of this. Young people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who have just purchased a home stand to be hurt \nby the significantly more than older homeowners  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who statistically have more equity if not a fully \npaid off house. But young people are still by far  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most supportive demographic of these policy \nchanges. Now, to play devil's advocate a bit,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there is one growing sector of the economy that \nmay be hurt by this market correction more than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most people are realizing, and that is aged \ncare. Over the last two decades, in particular,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107520,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this industry has grown up around effectively \nharvesting the equity from people's homes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1111520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to fund slightly more comfortable end of life \ncare. Reverse mortgages, cash out refinancing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1116800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or just selling the family house outright to pay \nfor a spot in a facility. There are a lot of old  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121760,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people as the baby boomers enter the twilight \nyears and things like age care facilities have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1126080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "become incredibly expensive because well \nthey could. Residents have been showing up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with houses that doubled or tripled in value over \nthe last 20 years. So the facilities raised their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1135360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rates to match their increased spending power. \nA lot of these facilities are heavily leveraged  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "themselves as very common targets for private \nequity leverage buyouts. They have historically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1144240,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been able to borrow against the assumption that a \nsteady stream of homeowners would keep showing up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ready to sell their house for six or seven figures \nto fund their stay. Now, nobody is going to shed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many tears if this particular industry gets \nstarved of its easy money from home equity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it's also worth realizing that for a lot of \nsmaller communities, age care has been one of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1162640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more stable sources of employment for a very long \ntime. More broadly, it would also be irresponsible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1167600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to not at least mention the wealth effect of \nnumber going up and the consumer pessimism that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1172080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "comes from number going down. A lot of people are \nspending based on the fact that they have a fully  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1176480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paid off house that has doubled in value in the \nlast 5 years. Sometimes even with the help of a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1181360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cash out refinance, which has always ended \nsuper well. It is irrational, but when the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1187360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number on Zillow drops by 15%, the instinct to \ntighten the belt follows pretty quickly. A stock  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1192800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "portfolio dropping is one thing, but for a lot of \nAmerican households, their house is their primary  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1197760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wealth. And unlike stocks, you can't sell 10% of \nyour house to cover expenses. Now, to be fair,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1203280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most economists who have weighed in on this, are \nnot predicting a 2008 style collapse from any of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1207920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these policy changes. Lending standards, despite \nour best attempts, are still significantly better  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1212960,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than they were back then. The securitization \nrisk is lower, and the underlying demand for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1217200,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "housing in this country is still very real. Now, \nall in all, this might all sound pretty good,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1222480,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it gets better because there are already \nother countries ahead of us in this process  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1226640,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we have had the luxury of watching from the \nsidelines. Chinese real estate was once the single  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1231280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "largest asset class on the planet. And since the \ngovernment introduced the three red lines policy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1235840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to crack down on excessively borrowing by property \ndevelopers in 2020, prices have been falling more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1240800,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or less consistently for a half decade at this \npoint. In the first quarter of this year alone,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1245040,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "residential sales fell 13.1% by area and 18.5% \nby value compared to the same period last year.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1251840,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Everrand, once the country's biggest developer, \ndelisted from the Hong Kong stock exchange,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1256400,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "making its shares worthless. Hundreds of \ndevelopment projects across the country were left  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1260560,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "half finishedish with deposits already collected \nfrom families who will probably never see these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1264880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "homes completed. Despite a very major disruption \nto the core asset class of their household wealth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1269520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Chinese economy is once again growing, \nonly this time being fueled by more sustainable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1274240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "industries like high-end manufacturing and exports \nrather than building and trading empty houses back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1278800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and forth to one another. This is despite other \nheadwinds like poor demographics, weak consumer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1283840,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confidence, and the ongoing tech crackdown \nin addition to their housing crash. So yeah,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1288320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if China had a housing market that was arguably \neven more important to their economy, crashed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1292720,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more severely, and was experienced alongside other \nheadwinds, and they have largely kept on chugging,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1297120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and if anything, realigned their economy \ninto more sustainable, productive industries,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1301600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's not been a painless experience, but it also \nwasn't the global financial crisis. My good friend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1307120,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yuri from Money and Macro made a video on \nthis as I was putting this one together,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1311360,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so I will leave a link to his work in the video \ndescription below. New Zealand is another one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1315600,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worth watching. After years of being one of the \nmost overheated housing markets on the planet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1319840,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the government cut building restrictions, raise \ninterest rates and adjusted tax concessions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1323760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and inflation adjusted prices in some major cities \ndropped by over a third from their peak. They've  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1328480,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pushed through even more reforms since, including \na law that lets homeowners build granny flats in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1333120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their backyards without any building consents at \nall. Housing inventory is now at a 10-year high,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1338480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it's very much a buyer market. Canada is \nalso worth considering because we are obviously  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1343280,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very close geographically, culturally, and \neconomically, and their housing market has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1347520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also kind of been going through it. Condo prices \nare down about 6.6% year-over-year nationally.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1353200,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the situation in Toronto and Vancouver is \nparticularly rough. In the first quarter of this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1357440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "year, there were over 4200 new completed condos \nsitting unsold in the greater Toronto area alone,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1362960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a record high. Vancouver has a similar glut with \nunsold inventory up 76% from a year ago. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1369760,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uh in the interest of fairness, there has been \npain and Canadian policy makers are starting to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1374320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get some cold feet. Prime Minister Carney recently \nannounced a billion half dollar program to buy up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1379360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unsold Vancouver condos and convert them into \nrent to own affordable housing, which a lot of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1384080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people are probably correctly just seeing as a \ndeveloper bailout sold to the general public as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1389280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an affordable housing program. The government says \nthe developers won't turn a profit on these sales,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1394320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is something, but they also won't be exposed \nto the catastrophic loss they would have endured  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1398720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had the government not been there to socialize \ntheir losses. And then there is Australia,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1403520,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "potentially the most cooked housing market on the \nplanet. They're phasing out negative gearing on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1407840,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "established property starting next year, which was \nthe tax loophole that let property investors ride  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1412640,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off rental losses against their salary income. \nWe always talk about housing as an investment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1417040,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conflicting with housing as shelter. But negative \ngearing in Australia meant that houses were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1421120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neither shelter nor assets so much as they were \ntax advantage financial instruments. But anyway,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1426320,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they are joining these other countries with \nsignificant housing reforms and they're  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1429760,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "additionally overhauling the capital gains tax \ndiscount to significantly reduce the incentive to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1434160,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "treat housing purely as a speculative investment. \nThese were once the supply side and investor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1438800,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deterrent policies that were once considered \nuntouchable in Australian politics. Now, it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1443040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worth noting that outside of China, all of these \ncountries had economies that were already slowing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1447520,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before their housing corrections really kicked in, \nmaking it genuinely difficult to separate cause  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1451920,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from effect. And again, this is all happening \nin a market with a lot of other stuff going on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1456000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the moment. So, go and watch this video next \nto find out where the housing market was before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1460400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we doubled down. And don't forget to like and \nsubscribe to keep on learning how money works."
    } ]
  } ]
}
